this may not look like much but for the  people of High Rock it means everything  this hilltop community on the island of  Grand Bahama lost its clinic in  hurricane durian the building flattened  but now for the people here there is a  temporary clinic staffed by 20  volunteers from the International  Medical Corps we have doctors nurses  medicines mental health experts and  we're seeing things that we commonly see  at this point and it recovery  lacerations people that are running out  of medications chronic conditions  turning to acute conditions and wound  care a lot of wound care getting giving  out tetanus as well as people come back  and are trying to clean up what's left  of their homes the International Medical  Corps has committed to be here for at  least three months it knows the facility  is pretty basic but the reality is it  will save lives  among those helping out miron rule a  former American football star he's now a  neurosurgeon in Boston but he was born  in the Bahamas and that's what's pulled  him back to help as when I saw the  hurricane hit being up in Boston and my  nice apartments at Harvard you know I  was I said man I have to do something  for my people for my country this means  a lot to me this country has taught me  much and I visited every summer as a  child you know I was christened here as  baptized here I'm on a national stamp I  mean this country is tied within me and  and so I called our staff at  Massachusetts General Hospital if there  was a global disaster team that was  going to the Bahamas I want to be a part  of it one big concern now is disease in  the days and weeks after a disaster it  can be a killer the clinic makes that  less likely and reminds people here they  haven't been forgotten Allen Fisher al  Jazeera High Rock on the island of Grand  Bahama
 

